  "id": "halcyontowers",
  "neon": "#39f0ff",
  "note": "Twenty-one floors, the second-tallest thing in Coldwater and the west side’s signature tower — and twenty-four axis-aligned boxes, which read as a stack of shoeboxes however many of them there were. It TWISTS now: five storeys each rotated about seven and a half degrees on the one below, so the corners walk round the building and the glass catches the light at a different angle on every set-back, finishing in a chamfered crown and a mast. ⚠ A YAWED BOX STILL LIGHTS ITSELF, which is the whole reason a tower can be built this way: `derivedTrim` refuses a rotated segment, so there is no derived kit above the podium, but the box still goes through `draw3DBoxAt` and keeps its curtain-wall texture and its lit floors. A drum in the same place would be a black column. ⚠ AND NONE OF THE TWISTED SEGMENTS IS OFFSET — see the warning in aurelia.json: a segment that is both rotated AND shifted puts the derived kit where its own solid is not, and glself catches it. RENDER_TUNE.legacyArms puts the luxtower arm back.",
